```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/phenotypic-covariance-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

`lm_phen_cov()` estimates the phenotypic covariance matrix `P` after the fixed
effects. There is no relatedness, so no `G` and no breeding values. This
vignette fits all 35 log-transformed cranial distances of the mouse data. The
distances are strongly integrated (they share one size gradient), so the traits
are first reparametrised with `size_shape()`, and every draw is mapped back to
the log-trait scale with `back_transform()`.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
library(patchwork)
```

## Size and shape scores

Keeping all `k - 1` shape axes makes the reparametrisation exact; a smaller
`n_shape` gives a lower-rank, and much cheaper, model.

```{r size-shape}
ratonesdf <- ratones_morpho
traits <- names(ratonesdf)[12:46]
log_traits <- log(as.matrix(ratonesdf[, traits]))
ss <- size_shape(log_traits)
ratonesdf <- cbind(ratonesdf, ss$scores)
c(measured_traits = length(traits), model_traits = ncol(ss$scores))
```

`animal_design()` builds `Y` and `X` from a formula. With many traits, the
`cbind()` response is assembled from the column names.

```{r design}
ratones_formula <- stats::reformulate(
  c("SEX", "AGE", "LIN"),
  response = sprintf("cbind(%s)", paste(ss$traits, collapse = ", ")))
ratones <- animal_design(ratones_formula, data = ratonesdf)
Y <- ratones$Y
X <- ratones$X
dim(Y)
```

## Fit

The LKJ prior acts on correlations between size and the shape axes, which are
principal components and so expected to be nearly uncorrelated; a firmer
`lkj_prior = 6` says so.

```{r fit, message = FALSE}
cov_fit <- lm_phen_cov(Y, X, lkj_prior = 6, iter = 800, warmup = 400,
                       chains = 4, cores = 4, refresh = 0, seed = 1)
cov_fit$summary(c("sigma_P[1]", "P[1,1]", "P[2,2]", "corrP[1,2]"))
cov_fit$diagnostic_summary()
```

## Back to the trait scale

`back_transform()` returns a list of arrays indexed by trait first and draw last:
`P` and `corrP` are traits by traits by draws and `sigma_P` is traits by draws,
all named after the measured traits.

```{r back-transform}
post <- back_transform(cov_fit, ss)
post
round(draws_mean(post, "sigma_P")[1:5], 3)
round(draws_mean(post, "corrP")[1:3, 1:3], 3)
```

The posterior mean `P` on the log-trait scale is close to the covariance of the
least-squares residuals, the unpenalised estimate of the same quantity:

```{r compare}
P_hat <- draws_mean(post, "P")
P_ols <- cov(residuals(lm(log_traits ~ X - 1)))
round(P_hat[1:4, 1:4], 4)
round(P_ols[1:4, 1:4], 4)
evolqg::MatrixCompare(P_hat, P_ols)
```

```{r corr, fig.width = 10, fig.height = 5}
plot_corr(cov2cor(P_hat), "Posterior mean phenotypic correlations") +
  plot_corr(cov2cor(P_ols), "Residual correlations")
```
